WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.026
Data pipelines can turn the rows of a file into content items.

00:00:05.026 --> 00:00:08.933
In this video, you import a list of products from a CSV file

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:13.255
in the media library, check the result, and run the import a second

00:00:13.255 --> 00:00:15.090
time to update the items.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.835
An import needs three things:

00:00:21.835 --> 00:00:26.310
a file in the media library, a content type that receives the rows,

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.988
and a pipeline that reads the file and saves each row.

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:33.420
The formats include CSV files and Excel workbooks.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.995
The pipeline of an import has two steps.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:40.128
Read a media file reads the rows of the file.

00:00:40.128 --> 00:00:44.980
Save content items creates or updates one content item for each row, with

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.700
the values you choose.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:51.230
Save content items treats a row in one of three ways.

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:55.842
It updates the item a row matches or creates one, it creates items

00:00:55.842 --> 00:01:00.390
and skips matches, or it only updates and rejects the other rows.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:08.882
You find the pipelines under Tools, Data Pipelines, Pipelines.

00:01:08.882 --> 00:01:12.150
The list is empty, so let's click Add Pipeline.

00:01:12.601 --> 00:01:14.558
A pipeline starts with a name.

00:01:14.558 --> 00:01:17.331
Let's type Import products, and click Create.

00:01:17.331 --> 00:01:18.961
The designer opens next.

00:01:19.418 --> 00:01:22.722
An empty pipeline offers the sources to start with.

00:01:22.722 --> 00:01:26.663
Let's choose Read a media file, which reads the rows of a file

00:01:26.663 --> 00:01:28.228
in the media library.

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:32.672
In the File box, we enter the path of the file in the media library:

00:01:32.672 --> 00:01:35.256
the imports folder, and the products file.

00:01:35.256 --> 00:01:40.209
The format comes from the extension, and the first row holds the names of the columns.

00:01:40.677 --> 00:01:42.872
The Data tab previews the rows.

00:01:42.872 --> 00:01:47.023
After a click on Preview, the five rows of the file appear, with

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:50.877
the columns Name, Price and Category, all of the type Text.

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:57.984
Next, we add the step that saves the rows.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:01.649
A click on the output dot of Read a media file lists the

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:05.727
steps that can come after it, and we choose Save content items.

00:02:06.196 --> 00:02:09.823
The Content type list holds the content types of the site.

00:02:09.823 --> 00:02:13.790
Let's choose Product, which has a title, a Price and a Category.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:18.776
The user who publishes the pipeline must be allowed to edit and publish its items.

00:02:19.227 --> 00:02:22.471
Once a type is chosen, the other settings appear.

00:02:22.471 --> 00:02:26.540
Rows decides what happens to each row, and we keep the default:

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:29.547
update the item a row matches, or create one.

00:02:30.009 --> 00:02:32.952
Match on tells how a row finds its item:

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:37.238
by content item id, or by display text, which is how the item

00:02:37.238 --> 00:02:38.773
is named in lists.

00:02:38.773 --> 00:02:43.379
The file has no ids, so we choose Display text and the field Name.

00:02:43.836 --> 00:02:48.996
Values connects the fields of the rows to the parts and fields of the items.

00:02:48.996 --> 00:02:50.821
Let's add three values:

00:02:50.821 --> 00:02:55.666
Name goes to Title, Price goes to Price, and Category goes to Category.

00:02:59.935 --> 00:03:05.625
Publish the items stays checked, so the items are published rather than saved as drafts.

00:03:05.625 --> 00:03:07.986
Items per save keeps its default.

00:03:07.986 --> 00:03:11.315
The Issues tab shows that nothing is left to fix.

00:03:11.778 --> 00:03:16.432
The Data tab of Save content items previews what the step would receive.

00:03:16.432 --> 00:03:20.728
A preview never runs a destination, so no content item is created.

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:28.844
Runs use the published version of a pipeline, so let's click Publish.

00:03:28.844 --> 00:03:32.821
A published pipeline runs with the access of the user who published it.

00:03:33.282 --> 00:03:34.566
Let's click Run.

00:03:34.566 --> 00:03:40.052
The History tab lists the run, and its status changes to Succeeded when the import ends.

00:03:40.504 --> 00:03:43.899
A click on Open the run shows the page of the run.

00:03:43.899 --> 00:03:47.232
Under Delivered, the pipeline reports the result:

00:03:47.232 --> 00:03:51.354
five content items created, none updated, and no row rejected.

00:03:55.564 --> 00:04:00.267
Under Content, Content Items, the list filtered on the Product type shows the

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:03.724
five products of the run, each one named after its row.

00:04:04.174 --> 00:04:08.030
Opening one of them shows its price and its category, taken from the

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.384
columns of the file.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.298
To import again, we go back to the list of pipelines.

00:04:13.298 --> 00:04:17.810
The Actions menu of the pipeline has an item that starts a run at once.

00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:24.171
This time every title already exists, so the five products are updated and none is created.

00:04:24.171 --> 00:04:28.007
Matching on the display text is what prevents duplicates.

00:04:32.207 --> 00:04:36.964
Each item is validated before it is saved, as the editor would validate it.

00:04:36.964 --> 00:04:41.663
A row that fails leaves through the Rejected output of Save content items,

00:04:41.663 --> 00:04:44.307
with the reason in a field named Error.

00:04:45.057 --> 00:04:49.452
To keep those rows, connect Rejected to Create a file, and then to

00:04:49.452 --> 00:04:51.406
Save to the media library.

00:04:51.406 --> 00:04:55.679
The result is a CSV file of the rejected rows and their reasons,

00:04:55.679 --> 00:04:57.877
ready to fix and import again.

00:04:59.877 --> 00:05:03.746
A data pipeline reads the rows of a file, saves them as content

00:05:03.746 --> 00:05:06.719
items, and updates those items on the next run.

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:08.177
Thanks for watching.

